1
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:13,814
Propose and reject gives every job its optimal candidate, the best partner it has in any stable matching.

2
00:00:13,814 --> 00:00:20,362
In our example that is the green matching, and the purple one is the only other stable matching.

3
00:00:20,516 --> 00:00:23,216
Now look at the columns of the candidates.

4
00:00:23,216 --> 00:00:27,886
For Cleo and for Dora the green job sits below the purple one.

5
00:00:27,886 --> 00:00:34,844
So the matching that is best for every job gives these two the lowest job they have in any stable matching,

6
00:00:34,844 --> 00:00:36,470
which we called pessimal.

7
00:00:36,650 --> 00:00:42,624
Ada and Bea have the same job in both, so for them best and worst are one and the same.

8
00:00:42,624 --> 00:00:46,094
Is this an accident of the example, or a law?

9
00:00:46,094 --> 00:00:50,912
Let us test it on Cleo, whose green job is job three.

10
00:00:52,083 --> 00:00:58,139
Suppose there were some other stable matching in which Cleo has a job she likes less than job three.

11
00:00:58,139 --> 00:01:02,859
On her list that could only be job one or job four.

12
00:01:03,016 --> 00:01:07,912
In that matching job three is not with Cleo, so it has some other candidate.

13
00:01:07,912 --> 00:01:10,498
Could she be above Cleo on its list?

14
00:01:10,498 --> 00:01:14,576
No, because Cleo is the optimal candidate of job three,

15
00:01:14,576 --> 00:01:19,692
the best it gets in any stable matching, and this matching is stable.

16
00:01:19,850 --> 00:01:25,220
So that other candidate is below Cleo, and job three would rather have Cleo.

17
00:01:25,220 --> 00:01:30,312
And Cleo would rather have job three than her job there, because that is what we supposed.

18
00:01:30,312 --> 00:01:35,354
They are a rogue couple, so that matching is not stable after all.

19
00:01:35,516 --> 00:01:39,302
So no stable matching gives Cleo less than job three.

20
00:01:39,302 --> 00:01:47,058
Job three is the worst she can get, her pessimal job, and the job optimal matching hands her exactly that.

21
00:01:47,216 --> 00:01:50,176
The argument never used the name of Cleo.

22
00:01:50,176 --> 00:01:55,792
Take any candidate, and call the job she gets in the job optimal matching her green job.

23
00:01:55,792 --> 00:01:59,878
Suppose some stable matching gave her a job she likes less.

24
00:01:59,878 --> 00:02:06,880
Then her green job has another partner there, and it likes her more, because she is its optimal candidate.

25
00:02:07,050 --> 00:02:12,678
So she and her green job would both rather have each other, and that matching has a rogue couple.

26
00:02:12,678 --> 00:02:16,874
So it does not exist, and her green job is her pessimal job.

27
00:02:16,874 --> 00:02:21,614
A job optimal matching is always candidate pessimal.

28
00:02:22,783 --> 00:02:28,619
So the side that proposes gets its best stable outcome, and the side that answers gets its worst.

29
00:02:28,619 --> 00:02:32,735
Then the remedy for the candidates is to let them do the proposing.

30
00:02:32,735 --> 00:02:36,358
None of our proofs cared which side was called jobs,

31
00:02:36,358 --> 00:02:40,609
so with candidates proposing the result is candidate optimal.

32
00:02:40,783 --> 00:02:42,565
Try it on our lists.

33
00:02:42,565 --> 00:02:49,529
Ada asks job one, Bea asks job four, Cleo asks job two, and Dora asks job three.

34
00:02:49,529 --> 00:02:57,123
Every job has exactly one offer, so it stops on the first day, and the result is the purple matching.

35
00:02:57,283 --> 00:03:01,115
This choice was made for real in the residency match.

36
00:03:01,115 --> 00:03:06,181
At first the hospitals did the proposing, so the result was hospital optimal.

37
00:03:06,181 --> 00:03:11,179
In the nineteen nineties the roles were reversed, so that the students do the proposing.

38
00:03:11,179 --> 00:03:17,915
Later changes also let married couples ask for positions at the same or nearby hospitals.

39
00:03:18,083 --> 00:03:23,584
The algorithm was in use for ten years before Gale and Shapley analysed it properly,

40
00:03:23,584 --> 00:03:25,877
in a paper from nineteen sixty-two.

41
00:03:25,877 --> 00:03:29,451
It always stops, it always ends in a stable matching,

42
00:03:29,451 --> 00:03:33,295
and it gives the best stable outcome to whoever proposes.

43
00:03:33,295 --> 00:03:38,591
So the question to ask of any matching system is who makes the offers.

